{
  "configuration_id": "config_010",
  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: The only completed experiment (config_001, RNA-only PCA-2000 single-profile) yields 0.485 accuracy, establishing a floor but leaving the most fundamental question unanswered: does the image modality carry useful signal for this task at all? Without knowing this, the search space splits into two very different strategies (RNA-focused vs. multimodal), and ~30 of the 51 configurations become irrelevant if image is uninformative. 2) What this tests: config_010 (image-only, single-profile, ResNet50) isolates the modality question with the simplest possible image pipeline, directly comparable in structural complexity to config_001. It tells us whether image features alone can approach, match, or exceed the RNA-only baseline, which determines whether multimodal fusion is worth pursuing and which modality should be the anchor. 3) Why best use of budget: With only one data point, the highest-value experiment is the one that most sharply reduces uncertainty about a top-level design axis. The modality question is more fundamental than encoder choice or profile aggregation—its answer will prune or prioritize large swaths of the remaining search space, making every subsequent experiment more targeted. Testing a richer RNA encoder (e.g., scvi) or multiprofile aggregation would be informative but less decisive, since those improvements are only relevant within the RNA branch and do not resolve whether the image channel should be included at all."
}

